{"text": "In a blast furnace, iron is obtained by electrolysis.", "expl": "It is obtained by reduction with carbon monoxide."},
{"text": "Cracking increases the proportion of the petrol (gasoline) fraction.", "expl": "It breaks long hydrocarbon chains into shorter ones."},
{"text": "Hydrogen is in group 1, but it is not a metal.", "expl": "Hydrogen has 1 valence electron like the alkali metals, but it is a gaseous non-metal."},
{"text": "Most elements in the periodic table are metals.", "expl": "Metals make up about 4/5 of all elements – they fill the left and middle of the periodic table."},
{"text": "At room temperature, chlorine is a yellow-green gas.", "expl": "Chlorine is a yellow-green, toxic gas with a choking smell. Its name comes from the Greek 'chloros', meaning pale green."},
{"text": "The symbol Mg stands for manganese.", "expl": "Mg is magnesium. Manganese is Mn – they are easy to mix up."},
{"text": "Mercury is the only metal that is liquid at room temperature.", "expl": "Mercury is a silvery, liquid and toxic metal. It used to be used in thermometers."},
{"text": "Graphite conducts electricity.", "expl": "Although graphite is a form of a non-metal (carbon), it conducts electricity, so it is used e.g. for electrodes."},
{"text": "Elements in the same group have the same number of electron shells.", "expl": "Elements in the same period have the same number of shells. Elements in a group share the same number of valence electrons."},
{"text": "Copper has a reddish colour.", "expl": "Copper is one of the few metals that is not silvery – it is reddish-brown."},
{"text": "Atoms of all noble gases have 8 valence electrons.", "expl": "Helium has only 2 electrons – its single, full first shell (K shell). The other noble gases have 8."},
{"text": "At room temperature, sulfur is a yellow solid.", "expl": "Sulfur is a brittle yellow solid. It does not conduct electricity, which is typical of non-metals."},
{"text": "At room temperature, iodine is a gas.", "expl": "Iodine is a dark violet solid. When heated, it easily turns into violet vapour (it sublimes)."},
{"text": "Aluminium has a lower density than iron.", "expl": "Aluminium's density is about 2.7 g/cm³ and iron's about 7.9 g/cm³, so light aluminium is used e.g. in aircraft and bicycles."},
{"text": "Gold rusts easily in moist air.", "expl": "Gold is a noble metal and hardly reacts with oxygen or water – that is why it stays shiny for centuries."},
{"text": "A cation forms when an atom loses electrons.", "expl": "After losing electrons, protons outnumber electrons, so the ion has a positive charge."},
{"text": "Isotopes of the same element have different numbers of protons.", "expl": "The number of protons determines which element it is. Isotopes differ in the number of neutrons."},
{"text": "The {HCl} molecule contains a polar covalent bond.", "expl": "Chlorine attracts the shared electron pair more strongly than hydrogen, but the difference is not big enough to form ions."},
{"text": "An atom is electrically neutral because it has as many protons as neutrons.", "expl": "Neutrons have no charge. An atom is neutral because it has as many protons (+) as electrons (−)."},
{"text": "Almost all the mass of an atom is concentrated in the nucleus.", "expl": "Protons and neutrons are about 1840 times heavier than electrons, and they are in the nucleus."},
{"text": "In compounds, hydrogen has a valency of 1.", "expl": "A hydrogen atom has only 1 electron, so it can form one bond – its valency is I."},
{"text": "The oxygen molecule {O2} contains an ionic bond.", "expl": "Both atoms are identical and attract electrons equally, so they form a non-polar covalent bond."},
{"text": "An argon atom has 8 valence electrons.", "expl": "Argon is in group 18 (18 − 10 = 8). Its full octet means it hardly reacts."},
{"text": "The mass number is the total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus.", "expl": "Mass number A = protons + neutrons, e.g. for sodium: 11 + 12 = 23. Electrons weigh almost nothing, so they are not counted."},
{"text": "In sodium oxide {Na2O}, sodium has a valency of 2.", "expl": "Oxygen (valency II) bonds to 2 sodium atoms, so each sodium atom has a valency of I."},
{"text": "The nucleus is many times smaller than the whole atom.", "expl": "The nucleus is tens of thousands of times smaller than the atom – most of an atom is space occupied by electrons."},
{"text": "Deuterium and tritium are isotopes of helium.", "expl": "Deuterium and tritium are isotopes of hydrogen – they have 1 proton in the nucleus and differ in the number of neutrons."},
{"text": "A potassium atom has 4 electron shells.", "expl": "Potassium is in period 4, and the period number equals the number of electron shells."},
{"text": "In a decomposition reaction, one substance forms at least two new substances.", "expl": "For example {2H2O -> 2H2 + O2} – a compound breaks down into simpler substances."},
{"text": "Combustion is an endothermic reaction.", "expl": "Combustion releases heat and light to the surroundings, so it is exothermic."},
{"text": "The mass of the products of a reaction can be greater than the mass of the reactants.", "expl": "According to the law of conservation of mass, atoms are neither created nor destroyed in a reaction, so the masses are equal."},
{"text": "The equation {2H2O -> 2H2 + O2} shows a decomposition reaction.", "expl": "One substance – water – forms two simpler ones: hydrogen and oxygen."},
{"text": "The molecular mass of {O2} is 32 u (O = 16 u).", "expl": "An oxygen molecule has 2 atoms, so 2 × 16 u = 32 u."},
{"text": "When balancing an equation, you may change the subscripts in formulas.", "expl": "Changing a subscript changes the substance, e.g. {H2O} into {H2O2}. When balancing, you only change the coefficients."},
{"text": "Photosynthesis is an endothermic reaction.", "expl": "Photosynthesis needs energy from sunlight, which the plant absorbs."},
{"text": "Hydrogen is a flammable gas.", "expl": "Hydrogen burns to form water. A mixture of hydrogen and air can explode."},
{"text": "Oxygen is a flammable gas.", "expl": "Oxygen does not burn itself, but it supports combustion – a glowing splint relights in it."},
{"text": "{2CO2} represents 4 oxygen atoms in total.", "expl": "The coefficient 2 multiplies the whole molecule: 2 molecules × 2 O atoms = 4 oxygen atoms."},
{"text": "Boiling an egg is a chemical change.", "expl": "Heat denatures the proteins – a substance with new properties forms, and the change is permanent."},
{"text": "The law of conservation of mass was formulated by Dmitri Mendeleev.", "expl": "The law of conservation of mass was formulated by Lomonosov and Lavoisier. Mendeleev created the periodic table."},
{"text": "In a synthesis reaction, one reactant forms several products.", "expl": "That describes decomposition. In synthesis, several reactants form one product, e.g. {2Mg + O2 -> 2MgO}."},
{"text": "Every acid contains oxygen in its molecule.", "expl": "There are oxygen-free acids, e.g. hydrochloric acid {HCl} and hydrosulfuric acid {H2S}."},
{"text": "A solution with pH = 11 is basic (alkaline).", "expl": "pH values above 7 mean a basic solution – the higher the pH, the more strongly basic."},
{"text": "Hydrochloric acid is a solution of hydrogen chloride in water.", "expl": "Hydrogen chloride {HCl} is a gas. Only when it dissolves in water does it form hydrochloric acid."},
{"text": "Copper(II) hydroxide dissolves well in water.", "expl": "{Cu(OH)2} is insoluble – it precipitates as a blue solid."},
{"text": "When an acid reacts with a hydroxide, water is formed.", "expl": "{H^+} cations from the acid combine with {OH^-} anions from the hydroxide to form {H2O} molecules."},
{"text": "Phosphoric acid has the formula {H3PO4}.", "expl": "The phosphate group {PO4} has a valency of III, so it bonds to 3 hydrogen atoms."},
{"text": "Sulfuric acid is an oxygen-free acid.", "expl": "{H2SO4} contains 4 oxygen atoms, so it is an oxoacid."},
{"text": "The lower the pH, the more acidic the solution.", "expl": "A lower pH means more {H^+} ions in the solution, so it is more strongly acidic."},
{"text": "Sodium hydroxide is corrosive.", "expl": "{NaOH} damages skin and tissue, so we handle it wearing gloves and safety goggles."},
{"text": "Pure carbonic acid can be stored in a closed bottle.", "expl": "Carbonic acid is unstable and decomposes into water and {CO2}. It exists only in dilute solution."},
{"text": "Phenolphthalein is pink in a neutral solution.", "expl": "Phenolphthalein is colourless in neutral and acidic solutions. It turns pink only in bases."},
{"text": "Potassium hydroxide dissociates into {K^+} and {OH^-} ions.", "expl": "{KOH -> K^+ + OH^-}. Hydroxides dissociate into a metal cation and hydroxide anions."},
{"text": "Lemon juice has a pH greater than 7.", "expl": "Lemon juice contains citric acid and is acidic, so its pH is less than 7."},
{"text": "Ethene can polymerise to form polyethylene.", "expl": "Thanks to the double bond, many ethene molecules join into long polyethylene chains."},
{"text": "Ethane decolourises bromine water.", "expl": "Ethane has only single bonds, so it does not add bromine; bromine water is decolourised by unsaturated hydrocarbons."},
{"text": "Methane is the simplest alkane.", "expl": "A methane molecule {CH4} has only one carbon atom – it starts the homologous series of alkanes."},
{"text": "Acetic acid is a strong acid.", "expl": "Ethanoic (acetic) acid is a weak acid – in water it dissociates into ions only to a small extent."},
{"text": "A solution of ethanol in water is neutral.", "expl": "Although ethanol has an {-OH} group, it does not dissociate into ions in water, so it does not change indicator colours."},
{"text": "All fats are solid at room temperature.", "expl": "Vegetable oils, e.g. sunflower oil or olive oil, are liquid at room temperature."},
{"text": "Butter is a fat of animal origin.", "expl": "Butter is made from cream, i.e. from the fat in cow's milk."},
{"text": "Starch dissolves well in cold water.", "expl": "Starch practically does not dissolve in cold water; in hot water it forms a paste (a colloid)."},
{"text": "Glucose tastes sweet and dissolves well in water.", "expl": "Glucose is a simple sugar with many {-OH} groups, which help it dissolve easily in water."},
{"text": "Ethanol is produced by the alcoholic fermentation of glucose.", "expl": "Yeast converts glucose into ethanol and carbon dioxide – this is how wine is made, for example."},
{"text": "Ethanol is flammable.", "expl": "Ethanol burns with an almost colourless, bluish flame, so it is kept away from fire."},
{"text": "A propane molecule has 4 carbon atoms.", "expl": "Propane has 3 carbon atoms ({C3H8}); butane has 4."},
{"text": "Carboxylic acids contain the carboxyl group {-COOH}.", "expl": "It is the {-COOH} group that gives these compounds their acidic properties."},
{"text": "Sugar can still be dissolved in a saturated solution of table salt.", "expl": "The solution is saturated only with respect to salt; another substance, such as sugar, can still dissolve in it."},
{"text": "Percentage concentration is the mass of solute in 100 g of solvent.", "expl": "Percentage concentration refers to 100 g of solution, not solvent."},
{"text": "Adding water to a solution lowers its percentage concentration.", "expl": "The mass of solute stays the same while the mass of solution increases, so the concentration falls."},
{"text": "Milk is a true solution.", "expl": "Milk is a colloid (an emulsion) – fat droplets scatter light, which is why it is opaque."},
{"text": "Distillation can be used to obtain distilled water from tap water.", "expl": "Water evaporates and condenses in the condenser, while the dissolved salts stay in the flask."},
{"text": "Filtration can separate dissolved salt from water.", "expl": "Dissolved salt passes through the filter paper with the water; filtration only removes undissolved solids."},
{"text": "Brass is an alloy of copper and zinc.", "expl": "Alloys are mixtures; brass is yellow and harder than pure copper."},
{"text": "The solute in a solution can be a gas.", "expl": "For example, sparkling water contains dissolved carbon dioxide."},
{"text": "The solubility of all solids increases with temperature.", "expl": "True for most, but not all – e.g. the solubility of calcium hydroxide decreases."},
{"text": "In a 20% solution, there are 80 g of water for every 20 g of solute.", "expl": "100 g of a 20% solution contains 20 g of solute and 100 g − 20 g = 80 g of water."},
{"text": "Evaporating some of the water from a solution increases its percentage concentration.", "expl": "The amount of solute stays the same while the mass of solution decreases, so the concentration rises."},
{"text": "Seawater is a pure substance.", "expl": "Seawater is a mixture of water and many dissolved salts, mainly sodium chloride."},
{"text": "Oil and water form a homogeneous mixture.", "expl": "Oil does not dissolve in water and forms a separate layer – it is a heterogeneous mixture."},
{"text": "You must never taste chemicals in the lab.", "expl": "Many chemicals are toxic or corrosive, even if they look harmless."},
{"text": "A beaker is heated on a tripod with a gauze or ceramic mat, not directly in the flame.", "expl": "The gauze spreads the heat and stops the glass cracking from local overheating."},
{"text": "GHS pictograms are diamond-shaped, with a red border and a black symbol on a white background.", "expl": "This uniform design makes it possible to recognise a hazard warning on packaging in any country."},
{"text": "A test tube being heated is filled to the top with liquid.", "expl": "A test tube is filled to at most about 1/3 of its height so that boiling liquid does not spurt out."},
{"text": "A conical flask is also called an Erlenmeyer flask.", "expl": "It is named after the German chemist Emil Erlenmeyer; the conical shape reduces splashing."},
{"text": "When diluting concentrated acid, you pour water into the acid.", "expl": "Always pour acid into water – slowly, while stirring. The other way round, the mixture heats up violently and can spatter."},
{"text": "The mouth of a test tube being heated should point towards your neighbour.", "expl": "Always point the mouth of the test tube where nobody is standing – boiling liquid can suddenly spurt out."},
{"text": "Experiments with volatile, irritating substances are carried out in a fume cupboard (fume hood).", "expl": "The fume cupboard removes harmful vapours and gases so they do not reach your lungs."},
{"text": "Hot lab glassware often looks exactly the same as cold glassware.", "expl": "That is why heated vessels are handled with a holder or tongs and placed on a heat-resistant mat."},
{"text": "A lit burner can be left unattended if the flame is small.", "expl": "Never leave a lit burner unattended – even a small flame can start a fire."},
{"text": "Limewater turns cloudy (milky) with oxygen.", "expl": "Limewater turns cloudy with carbon dioxide; oxygen is detected with a glowing splint."},
{"text": "Chemicals are taken out with a clean spatula or pipette.", "expl": "A dirty spatula could contaminate the whole bottle of reagent and spoil later experiments."},
{"text": "The flame pictogram indicates flammable substances.", "expl": "Flammable substances, e.g. ethanol or acetone, are kept away from fire. A flame over a circle, on the other hand, means oxidising substances."},
{"text": "Carbon monoxide is odourless.", "expl": "Carbon monoxide cannot be smelled, which is why homes with stoves or boilers have CO detectors."},
{"text": "Methane is heavier than air.", "expl": "Methane is lighter than air, so when it leaks it collects near the ceiling."},
{"text": "Paper can be recycled an unlimited number of times.", "expl": "Each time it is recycled, the cellulose fibres get shorter, so the number of cycles is limited."},
{"text": "Polyethylene plastic bags break down in the environment within a few days.", "expl": "Polyethylene breaks down in nature very slowly – over decades or even hundreds of years."},
{"text": "Crude oil is a mixture of hydrocarbons.", "expl": "Crude oil contains many hydrocarbons with different boiling points, so it can be separated by distillation."},
{"text": "Coal is a renewable energy source.", "expl": "Coal formed over millions of years, so its reserves are effectively non-renewable."},
{"text": "Acid rain damages monuments made of limestone and marble.", "expl": "Acids react with calcium carbonate, which these rocks are made of."},
{"text": "Green plants take in carbon dioxide from the air.", "expl": "Plants use {CO2} in photosynthesis to make glucose."},
{"text": "Water hardness can be partly removed by boiling.", "expl": "During boiling, some calcium and magnesium compounds precipitate as limescale."},
{"text": "Smog is especially dangerous on windless days during the heating season.", "expl": "Without wind, pollution from chimneys and cars does not disperse and builds up over the city."},
{"text": "Aluminium cans cannot be recycled.", "expl": "Aluminium can be remelted many times, and recycling uses far less energy than producing it from ore."},
{"text": "Fossil fuels formed from the remains of ancient organisms.", "expl": "Coal, oil and natural gas formed over millions of years from the remains of plants and tiny marine organisms."},
{"text": "Burning hydrogen produces carbon dioxide.", "expl": "Hydrogen contains no carbon, so it burns to form only water: {2H2 + O2 -> 2H2O}."},
{"text": "In the main groups, elements of the same group have the same number of valence electrons.", "expl": "That is why they have similar chemical properties, e.g. all alkali metals have the valence configuration ns¹."},
{"text": "Sodium forms a cation more easily than magnesium because it has a lower first ionisation energy.", "expl": "Sodium has one valence electron and a smaller nuclear charge than magnesium in the same period, so it loses it more easily."},
{"text": "Oxygen and sulfur belong to the same group of the periodic table.", "expl": "Both are in group 16 (the chalcogens) and have 6 valence electrons (ns² np⁴)."},
{"text": "Aluminium belongs to the s-block.", "expl": "Aluminium has the valence configuration 3s² 3p¹ – the last electron enters a p subshell, so it is in the p-block (group 13)."},
{"text": "Chlorine is a stronger oxidising agent than iodine.", "expl": "Halogen reactivity decreases down the group, so chlorine displaces iodine from iodides: {Cl2 + 2KI -> 2KCl + I2}."},
{"text": "Metallic character increases across a period from left to right.", "expl": "It decreases: across a period the number of valence electrons and the electronegativity increase, so non-metals appear."},
{"text": "Although helium has the configuration 1s², it is placed in group 18, not group 2.", "expl": "Helium has a full valence shell and is chemically inert like the other noble gases, unlike the reactive group 2 metals."},
{"text": "Some d-block elements are non-metals.", "expl": "All d-block elements are transition metals, e.g. iron, copper, zinc, silver and gold."},
{"text": "Copper is an alkali metal.", "expl": "Copper is a transition metal in group 11 (d-block). The alkali metals are the group 1 elements except hydrogen."},
{"text": "The {Cl^-} anion has a larger radius than the chlorine atom.", "expl": "The extra electron increases electron–electron repulsion with the same nuclear charge, so the electron cloud expands."},
{"text": "The periodic table has 8 periods.", "expl": "The periodic table has 7 periods (horizontal rows) and 18 groups."},
{"text": "Fluorine can have a positive oxidation state in compounds.", "expl": "Fluorine is the most electronegative element, so in compounds its oxidation state is always −I."},
{"text": "Carbon belongs to the s-block.", "expl": "Carbon (2s² 2p²) is in group 14 and belongs to the p-block."},
{"text": "Two electrons in the same orbital must have opposite spins.", "expl": "This follows from the Pauli exclusion principle: no two electrons in an atom can have the same set of quantum numbers."},
{"text": "Isotopes of an element have clearly different chemical properties.", "expl": "Chemical properties are determined by the electron configuration, which is the same for all isotopes of an element."},
{"text": "Gamma radiation is deflected by an electric field.", "expl": "Gamma rays are electromagnetic waves (photons) with no charge, so an electric field does not deflect them."},
{"text": "In a methane molecule, all C–H bonds are equivalent.", "expl": "The carbon atom is sp³ hybridised; four identical orbitals form four identical bonds pointing to the corners of a tetrahedron."},
{"text": "The {CO2} molecule is polar.", "expl": "The C=O bonds are polar, but the molecule is linear and symmetrical, so the dipoles cancel out."},
{"text": "A neutron has a positive charge.", "expl": "A neutron is electrically neutral; the proton is positive and the electron is negative."},
{"text": "Ionic bonds usually form between elements with a large difference in electronegativity.", "expl": "With a large electronegativity difference (by convention above about 1.7 on the Pauling scale), electrons are essentially transferred from metal to non-metal, e.g. in {KF} or {NaCl}."},
{"text": "The mass of an electron is about 1840 times smaller than the mass of a proton.", "expl": "The proton-to-electron mass ratio is about 1836, which is why almost all of an atom's mass is in the nucleus."},
{"text": "Solid sodium chloride is a good conductor of electricity.", "expl": "In the crystal the ions are fixed in the lattice. {NaCl} conducts only when molten or dissolved in water."},
{"text": "sp² hybridisation gives a flat, trigonal arrangement of σ bonds.", "expl": "The three sp² orbitals lie in one plane at 120°, e.g. in {BF3} or around the C atoms in ethene."},
{"text": "Hydrogen bonds are stronger than typical covalent bonds.", "expl": "Hydrogen bonds are roughly 10–20 times weaker than covalent bonds, but stronger than ordinary van der Waals forces."},
{"text": "In alpha (α) decay, the mass number of the nucleus decreases by 4.", "expl": "The nucleus emits an α particle made of 2 protons and 2 neutrons, so it loses 4 nucleons."},
{"text": "In graphite, the carbon atoms are sp² hybridised.", "expl": "Each C atom bonds to three neighbours in flat layers, and the delocalised π electrons let graphite conduct electricity."},
{"text": "In the reaction {2Na + Cl2 -> 2NaCl}, chlorine is the oxidising agent.", "expl": "Chlorine gains electrons (0 → −1), so it is itself reduced while it oxidises sodium (0 → +1)."},
{"text": "At dynamic equilibrium, the forward and reverse reactions stop.", "expl": "Both reactions keep going, but at the same rate, so the concentrations no longer change."},
{"text": "The oxidation state of an uncombined element is 0.", "expl": "In an element, e.g. {O2}, {Fe}, {Cl2}, there is no electron shift between different elements."},
{"text": "1 mole of oxygen molecules {O2} has a mass of 16 g (O = 16).", "expl": "An {O2} molecule has two atoms: M = 2 · 16 = 32 g/mol. 16 g is the mass of 1 mole of oxygen atoms."},
{"text": "In an exothermic reaction, the enthalpy of the products is greater than that of the reactants.", "expl": "It is smaller: the system releases energy to the surroundings, so ΔH = H(products) − H(reactants) < 0."},
{"text": "The combustion of methane is exothermic.", "expl": "{CH4 + 2O2 -> CO2 + 2H2O} releases a lot of heat – that is why natural gas is used as a fuel."},
{"text": "At STP (0 °C, 1013 hPa), 2 moles of a gas (treated as ideal) occupy 44.8 dm³.", "expl": "The molar volume at STP is 22.4 dm³/mol, so 2 · 22.4 = 44.8 dm³."},
{"text": "In a redox reaction, the reducing agent gives away electrons.", "expl": "The reducing agent donates electrons to the oxidising agent and is itself oxidised (its oxidation state increases)."},
{"text": "Increasing the concentration of a reactant shifts the equilibrium towards the reactants.", "expl": "By Le Chatelier's principle the system uses up the extra reactant, so the equilibrium shifts towards the products."},
{"text": "In {Fe2O3}, iron has an oxidation state of +2.", "expl": "2x + 3 · (−2) = 0, so x = +3. This is iron(III) oxide."},
{"text": "In a chemical reaction, the mass of the reactants equals the mass of the products.", "expl": "This is the law of conservation of mass: atoms are not created or destroyed, only rearranged into new compounds."},
{"text": "In metal hydrides such as {NaH}, hydrogen has an oxidation state of −1.", "expl": "Hydrogen is more electronegative than sodium, so it gains an electron and forms the hydride ion {H^-}."},
{"text": "The rate of a reaction usually decreases as the reaction proceeds.", "expl": "Reactants are used up, so their concentration falls, and with it the number of collisions per unit time."},
{"text": "Carbonic acid is a stable acid.", "expl": "{H2CO3} easily decomposes into {CO2} and water – hence the bubbles in sparkling water."},
{"text": "A solution with pH = 7 at 25 °C is neutral.", "expl": "At 25 °C in a neutral solution [{H^+}] = [{OH^-}] = 10⁻⁷ mol/dm³, so pH = 7."},
{"text": "Copper(II) hydroxide is very soluble in water.", "expl": "{Cu(OH)2} is a blue, practically insoluble precipitate, formed e.g. in the reaction {CuSO4 + 2NaOH -> Cu(OH)2 + Na2SO4}."},
{"text": "According to Brønsted–Lowry theory, water can act as both an acid and a base.", "expl": "Water donates a proton (e.g. to ammonia, forming {OH^-}) or accepts one (e.g. from {HCl}, forming {H3O^+})."},
{"text": "Barium sulfate is very soluble in water.", "expl": "{BaSO4} is practically insoluble – that is why it can be safely given to patients as a contrast agent for X-rays."},
{"text": "Aqueous solutions of all salts are neutral.", "expl": "Salts of weak acids or weak bases undergo hydrolysis, e.g. {Na2CO3} gives an alkaline solution and {NH4Cl} an acidic one."},
{"text": "An aqueous solution of sodium acetate {CH3COONa} is alkaline.", "expl": "The anion of weak acetic acid takes a proton from water: {CH3COO^- + H2O <=> CH3COOH + OH^-}."},
{"text": "Electrolytic dissociation is caused by an electric current.", "expl": "An electrolyte splits into ions due to polar water molecules as soon as it dissolves; no current is needed."},
{"text": "Diluting an acid solution with water increases its pH.", "expl": "On dilution the concentration of {H^+} ions falls, so the pH rises (towards 7)."},
{"text": "Nitric acid is an oxygen-free acid (a hydracid).", "expl": "{HNO3} contains oxygen in its acid anion, so it is an oxoacid. Oxygen-free acids include {HCl} and {H2S}."},
{"text": "Sodium oxide reacts with water to form a base.", "expl": "{Na2O + H2O -> 2NaOH}: sodium oxide is a basic oxide."},
{"text": "The concentration of {H^+} ions in a solution with pH = 4 is 10 times greater than in a solution with pH = 3.", "expl": "It is 10 times smaller: at pH = 4 [{H^+}] = 10⁻⁴, and at pH = 3 [{H^+}] = 10⁻³ mol/dm³."},
{"text": "Glucose and fructose are isomers.", "expl": "Both have the formula {C6H12O6}; glucose is an aldose and fructose is a ketose."},
{"text": "Propanone (acetone) gives a positive Tollens' test.", "expl": "Ketones have no aldehyde group and do not reduce silver ions."},
{"text": "Alkanes readily undergo addition reactions.", "expl": "Alkanes have only single bonds, so they undergo substitution, not addition."},
{"text": "Ethyne decolourises bromine water.", "expl": "The triple bond adds bromine, just like the double bond in alkenes."},
{"text": "All the carbon atoms in a benzene molecule are sp² hybridised.", "expl": "A flat ring with 120° angles and delocalised π electrons requires sp² hybridisation."},
{"text": "An aqueous solution of ethanol turns methyl orange red.", "expl": "Ethanol hardly dissociates in water, so its solution is neutral."},
{"text": "Phenol reacts with {NaOH} solution.", "expl": "Phenol is weakly acidic and forms sodium phenoxide with the base."},
{"text": "Starch turns dark blue (blue-black) with iodine.", "expl": "Iodine molecules fit inside the amylose helix, forming a dark blue complex."},
{"text": "Cellulose is made of fructose units.", "expl": "Cellulose is a polymer of β-glucose units joined by glycosidic bonds."},
{"text": "Liquid vegetable fats (oils) contain mainly unsaturated fatty acid residues.", "expl": "C=C bonds bend the chains so they cannot pack tightly, which is why oils are liquid."},
{"text": "Hardening of fats is a hydrolysis reaction.", "expl": "Hardening is the catalytic hydrogenation of the double bonds (e.g. over nickel)."},
{"text": "Formalin is an approx. 40% aqueous solution of methanal.", "expl": "Formaldehyde (methanal) dissolves well in water; formalin is used e.g. to preserve biological specimens."},
{"text": "Ethanoic acid is a stronger acid than hydrochloric acid.", "expl": "Acetic acid is a weak acid and only slightly dissociated, while {HCl} dissociates almost completely."},
{"text": "A 0.1 mol/dm³ solution contains 0.1 mol of solute in 1 dm³ of solution.", "expl": "That is the definition of molar concentration: number of moles per 1 dm³ of solution."},
{"text": "Mist is an example of a foam.", "expl": "Mist is an aerosol (liquid droplets in a gas); a foam is a gas dispersed in a liquid."},
{"text": "Evaporating some of the water increases the percentage concentration of a solution.", "expl": "The mass of solute stays the same while the mass of the solution decreases."},
{"text": "Molar concentration is the number of moles of solute in 1 kg of solvent.", "expl": "That is the definition of molality; molar concentration refers to 1 dm³ of solution."},
{"text": "A 10% {NaCl} solution contains 10 g of {NaCl} in 100 g of water.", "expl": "It contains 10 g of {NaCl} in 100 g of solution, i.e. in 90 g of water."},
{"text": "A supersaturated solution is unstable.", "expl": "A shake or a seed crystal is enough to make the excess solute crystallise out."},
{"text": "Decanting means pouring a liquid off a sediment.", "expl": "Once the solid has settled, the liquid is carefully poured off; it is faster but less thorough than filtration."},
{"text": "Mixing 10% and 30% solutions of the same salt can give a 40% solution.", "expl": "The concentration of the mixture always lies between the concentrations of the solutions mixed."},
{"text": "Filtration can separate a salt dissolved in water.", "expl": "Dissolved ions pass through the filter paper; the salt is recovered by evaporating the water."},
{"text": "Egg white forms a colloidal solution with water.", "expl": "Large protein molecules are colloid-sized and show the Tyndall effect."},
{"text": "Sugar dissolves faster in hot tea than in cold tea.", "expl": "At higher temperature the particles move faster, which speeds up dissolving."},
{"text": "Mixing 1 dm³ of water with 1 dm³ of ethanol gives exactly 2 dm³ of solution.", "expl": "The volume contracts – hydrogen bonding lets the molecules pack more tightly."},
{"text": "Safety goggles only need to be worn when working with concentrated acids.", "expl": "Goggles are worn the whole time in the lab – splashes of alkalis, hot liquids and broken glass are hazards too."},
{"text": "The pictogram showing liquid pouring onto a hand and a metal bar means a corrosive substance.", "expl": "This is GHS05 – the substance destroys skin and may corrode metals."},
{"text": "A volumetric flask may be strongly heated over a Bunsen burner.", "expl": "Heating changes the volume of the glass and ruins its calibration; the flask is for making up solutions."},
{"text": "Hydrogen forms an explosive mixture with air.", "expl": "That is why hydrogen is tested for purity before it is lit."},
{"text": "In the lab you may taste sugar, because it is a food.", "expl": "No substance may ever be tasted in the lab – it may be contaminated."},
{"text": "A safety data sheet contains first-aid information.", "expl": "First-aid measures are one of the compulsory sections of a safety data sheet (SDS)."},
{"text": "A pipette is filled by sucking up the liquid by mouth.", "expl": "A pipette bulb or pipette filler is used to avoid poisoning or burns."},
{"text": "A fume cupboard (fume hood) has an extraction system.", "expl": "The extraction removes harmful gases and vapours from the work area."},
{"text": "Ammonia turns moist universal indicator paper red.", "expl": "Ammonia is alkaline, so it turns the paper blue or blue-green."},
{"text": "Copper compounds give a green flame.", "expl": "Copper ions colour the flame green or blue-green."},
{"text": "Leftover sodium metal can be thrown down the sink.", "expl": "Sodium reacts violently with water, releasing hydrogen, which may catch fire."},
{"text": "A measuring cylinder measures volume more accurately than a volumetric (bulb) pipette.", "expl": "A volumetric pipette is much more accurate; a measuring cylinder is for approximate measurements."},
{"text": "Hazard statement H225 means highly flammable liquid and vapour.", "expl": "H2xx statements cover physical hazards; ethanol, for example, carries H225."},
{"text": "The synthesis of ammonia from nitrogen and hydrogen is exothermic.", "expl": "The reaction releases heat, so too high a temperature shifts the equilibrium to the left."},
{"text": "A blast furnace produces pure iron with no impurities.", "expl": "The product is pig iron containing about 4% carbon plus silicon, manganese, phosphorus and sulfur."},
{"text": "Petrol (gasoline) is a single chemical compound.", "expl": "Petrol is a mixture of many hydrocarbons, mainly C5–C12."},
{"text": "Polyethylene biodegrades within a few weeks.", "expl": "Polyethylene breaks down in the environment extremely slowly – it can take hundreds of years."},
{"text": "PVC molecules contain chlorine atoms.", "expl": "Each PVC repeating unit is {-CH2-CHCl-}."},
{"text": "The main component of duralumin is copper.", "expl": "Duralumin is an aluminium alloy with a few percent of copper and magnesium – light and strong."},
{"text": "Gypsum is hydrated calcium sulfate.", "expl": "Gypsum is calcium sulfate dihydrate; when heated it loses some water to form plaster of Paris."},
{"text": "Coking of coal produces coke, coal tar and coke-oven gas.", "expl": "It is the destructive distillation of coal in the absence of air."},
{"text": "Stainless steel contains added chromium.", "expl": "Chromium forms a tight oxide layer on the surface that protects the steel from corrosion."},
{"text": "The main component of natural gas is propane.", "expl": "The main component of natural gas is methane; propane and butane make up LPG."},
{"text": "Carbon monoxide is an odourless, poisonous gas.", "expl": "{CO} binds to haemoglobin more strongly than oxygen, and having no smell makes it hard to detect."},
{"text": "Thermoplastics soften when heated and can be reshaped.", "expl": "This is why thermoplastics such as PE and PET can be reprocessed and recycled."},
{"text": "Superphosphate is a potassium fertiliser.", "expl": "Superphosphate contains {Ca(H2PO4)2}, so it is a phosphorus fertiliser."},
{"text": "The {Fe^2+} ion has the configuration [Ar] 4s² 3d⁴.", "expl": "Transition metals lose their 4s electrons first, so Fe²⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁶."},
{"text": "Aqueous copper(II) salt solutions are blue because the {Cu^2+} ion has an incompletely filled 3d subshell.", "expl": "Cu²⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁹ – d–d transitions are possible, and the hydrated ion absorbs red-orange light, so the solution looks blue."},
{"text": "The lanthanide contraction is the increase in atomic radius of the lanthanides with increasing atomic number.", "expl": "It is the opposite – the radii decrease, because 4f electrons shield the growing nuclear charge poorly."},
{"text": "All actinides are radioactive.", "expl": "No actinide has a stable isotope – all their nuclei undergo radioactive decay."},
{"text": "The {Sc^3+} ion forms coloured aqueous solutions.", "expl": "Sc³⁺ has the [Ar] configuration with no d electrons, so no d–d transitions occur and its solutions are colourless."},
{"text": "The first ionisation energy of magnesium is higher than that of aluminium.", "expl": "In aluminium the electron is removed from the higher-energy 3p subshell, in magnesium from the filled 3s² – hence the exception to the trend."},
{"text": "In the compound {[Co(NH3)6]Cl3}, cobalt has an oxidation state of +3.", "expl": "Ammonia is neutral and three {Cl^-} ions balance the 3+ charge of the complex, so cobalt is +3."},
{"text": "The coordination number of a central ion is always equal to its oxidation state.", "expl": "They are independent – e.g. in {[Fe(CN)6]^4-} iron is +2 but has a coordination number of 6."},
{"text": "Helium belongs to the s-block, even though it is placed in group 18.", "expl": "Helium is 1s² – its valence electrons are in an s subshell, but it sits in group 18 because it behaves as a noble gas."},
{"text": "Oxides of transition metals in high oxidation states, e.g. {Mn2O7} and {CrO3}, are acidic.", "expl": "A high oxidation state makes the bonds more covalent; with water they form acids – {HMnO4} and chromic acids."},
{"text": "The radius of the {Na^+} ion is larger than the radius of a sodium atom.", "expl": "The cation loses its whole 3s shell and the remaining electrons are pulled in more strongly, so Na⁺ is much smaller than the atom."},
{"text": "Molybdenum has the configuration [Kr] 5s² 4d⁴.", "expl": "Like chromium, molybdenum has an electron 'promotion': [Kr] 5s¹ 4d⁵ (two half-filled subshells)."},
{"text": "Fluorine has an oxidation state of −1 in all its compounds.", "expl": "As the most electronegative element, fluorine always pulls the bonding pair towards itself; it is 0 only in elemental {F2}."},
{"text": "Isotopes of the same element differ in the number of neutrons in the nucleus.", "expl": "Isotopes have the same number of protons (Z) but a different mass number A, i.e. a different number of neutrons."},
{"text": "The spin quantum number can have the value 0.", "expl": "The spin quantum number of an electron is ½, and the spin magnetic quantum number can only be +½ or −½ – never 0."},
{"text": "The oxygen atom in an {H2O} molecule is sp³ hybridised.", "expl": "Oxygen has 2 bonding pairs and 2 lone pairs – four electron domains correspond to sp³ hybridisation."},
{"text": "The {SO3} molecule is polar.", "expl": "{SO3} is trigonal planar (sp², no lone pair on S), so the bond dipoles cancel out."},
{"text": "In a potassium atom the 4s subshell is lower in energy than 3d, so it fills first.", "expl": "By the Madelung (n + l) rule: for 4s the sum is 4, for 3d it is 5, so 4s is filled before 3d."},
{"text": "Emission of gamma (γ) radiation changes the atomic number of a nucleus.", "expl": "A γ ray is a photon with no charge or mass – the nucleus only loses energy, and Z and A stay the same."},
{"text": "A triple bond consists of one σ bond and two π bonds.", "expl": "The first bond forms by head-on orbital overlap (σ), the other two by sideways overlap of p orbitals (π)."},
{"text": "The half-life of a radioactive isotope depends on the temperature of the sample.", "expl": "Radioactive decay happens in the nucleus and is practically unaffected by external conditions such as temperature or pressure."},
{"text": "The {PCl3} molecule is trigonal planar.", "expl": "Phosphorus has 3 bonding pairs and 1 lone pair, so the molecule is trigonal pyramidal (like {NH3})."},
{"text": "The bond energy of a C≡C triple bond is greater than that of a C–C single bond.", "expl": "More shared electron pairs mean a shorter, stronger bond (about 837 vs 348 kJ/mol)."},
{"text": "Electron capture by a nucleus decreases the atomic number by 1.", "expl": "The nucleus captures an electron from the K shell and a proton turns into a neutron: Z falls by 1, A is unchanged."},
{"text": "sp hybrid orbitals are at an angle of 180°.", "expl": "The two sp orbitals lie on a straight line on opposite sides of the atom – as in {CO2} or {C2H2}."},
{"text": "The carbon atom in methanal {HCHO} is sp³ hybridised.", "expl": "Carbon forms a C=O double bond and two C–H bonds – three bonding domains mean sp² hybridisation."},
{"text": "Adding a catalyst increases the amount of product at equilibrium.", "expl": "A catalyst speeds up the forward and reverse reactions equally – equilibrium is reached faster, but its composition does not change."},
{"text": "In a galvanic (voltaic) cell, the cathode is the positive electrode.", "expl": "Reduction occurs at the cathode, taking electrons from the external circuit – so in a galvanic cell it is the positive pole."},
{"text": "During electrolysis, oxidation takes place at the cathode.", "expl": "Reduction always takes place at the cathode and oxidation at the anode – in both galvanic and electrolytic cells."},
{"text": "A charge of 96 500 C always deposits 1 mole of any metal.", "expl": "96 500 C is 1 mole of electrons; e.g. for {Cu^2+} it deposits only 0.5 mol of copper, and for {Al^3+} only 1/3 mol of aluminium."},
{"text": "The standard enthalpy of formation of an element in its stable form is zero.", "expl": "This is true by definition, e.g. for {O2}(g) or graphite – it is the reference point for thermochemical calculations."},
{"text": "For a spontaneous reaction at constant temperature and pressure, ΔG < 0.", "expl": "A negative Gibbs free energy change means the process can occur spontaneously under these conditions."},
{"text": "A temperature rise of 10 K usually increases the reaction rate 2–4 times.", "expl": "This is van 't Hoff's rule: at higher temperature more molecules have at least the activation energy."},
{"text": "In the reaction {2H2O2 -> 2H2O + O2}, oxygen is both oxidised and reduced.", "expl": "This is disproportionation: oxygen goes from −1 to −2 (in water) and to 0 (in {O2})."},
{"text": "Raising the temperature always increases the value of the equilibrium constant.", "expl": "K increases with temperature only for endothermic reactions; for exothermic reactions it decreases."},
{"text": "In the ideal gas equation (pV = nRT), temperature is given in kelvins.", "expl": "pV = nRT needs absolute temperature; using degrees Celsius gives wrong (e.g. zero) volumes."},
{"text": "The half-life of a first-order reaction does not depend on the initial concentration.", "expl": "For first order t½ = ln2 / k – it depends only on the rate constant, not on the reactant concentration."},
{"text": "Removing a product from the system shifts the equilibrium towards the reactants.", "expl": "By Le Chatelier's principle the system replaces the lost product, so the equilibrium shifts towards the products."},
{"text": "The potential of the standard hydrogen electrode is defined as 0 V.", "expl": "It is the reference electrode – standard potentials of other half-cells are given relative to it."},
{"text": "An aqueous solution of {NaHSO4} is acidic.", "expl": "The {HSO4^-} ion is a fairly strong acid and dissociates into {H^+} and {SO4^2-}, making the solution acidic."},
{"text": "At the equivalence point in the titration of a weak acid with a strong base, the pH is 7.", "expl": "A salt of a weak acid forms, and its anion hydrolyses, so the pH at the equivalence point is above 7."},
{"text": "The dissociation constant of a weak acid depends on its concentration.", "expl": "Ka depends only on temperature; it is the degree of dissociation α that depends on concentration (Ostwald's dilution law)."},
{"text": "Adding {AgNO3} to a saturated {AgCl} solution decreases the solubility of {AgCl}.", "expl": "Common-ion effect: the extra {Ag^+} ions shift the dissolution equilibrium towards the precipitate."},
{"text": "The solubility product changes when a salt with a common ion is added.", "expl": "Ksp is a constant (it depends on temperature). The salt's solubility changes, not the value of Ksp."},
{"text": "A solution of a salt containing {Al^3+} ions is acidic.", "expl": "The hydrated aluminium ion donates protons to water molecules (cation hydrolysis), e.g. in {AlCl3} solution."},
{"text": "The degree of dissociation of a weak acid increases when the solution is diluted.", "expl": "By Ostwald's dilution law (Ka ≈ α²·c), at constant Ka a lower concentration means a larger α."},
{"text": "Ammonia is a Lewis base.", "expl": "The nitrogen atom in {NH3} has a lone pair it can donate to an acceptor, e.g. {H^+} or {BF3}."},
{"text": "For a conjugate acid–base pair at 25 °C, pKa + pKb = 14.", "expl": "This follows from Ka·Kb = Kw = 10⁻¹⁴ after taking logarithms."},
{"text": "A buffer has its greatest buffer capacity when the concentrations of its components are equal.", "expl": "At a 1:1 ratio (pH = pKa) the buffer neutralises added acid and added base equally well."},
{"text": "A solution with pH = 4 has a 100 times greater {H^+} concentration than a solution with pH = 6.", "expl": "The pH scale is logarithmic: a difference of 2 units is a 10² = 100-fold difference in concentration."},
{"text": "Phosphoric acid dissociates more strongly in its second and third steps than in the first.", "expl": "It is the opposite: Ka1 > Ka2 > Ka3, because it is harder to remove a proton from an increasingly negative anion."},
{"text": "An aqueous solution of {Na2S} is acidic.", "expl": "The {S^2-} ion comes from a weak acid and hydrolyses, taking protons from water – the solution is alkaline."},
{"text": "Bromination of benzene in the presence of {FeBr3} is electrophilic substitution.", "expl": "{FeBr3} polarises the {Br2} molecule, creating an electrophile that replaces a hydrogen atom on the ring."},
{"text": "Addition of {HBr} to propene in the presence of peroxides is anti-Markovnikov.", "expl": "Peroxides change the mechanism to a radical one; Br adds to C1 and 1-bromopropane forms."},
{"text": "The SN1 mechanism proceeds via a carbanion.", "expl": "In SN1 the leaving group departs first, forming a carbocation, which is then attacked by the nucleophile."},
{"text": "A meso compound is optically active.", "expl": "A meso compound has an internal plane of symmetry, so despite its stereocentres it is achiral."},
{"text": "Enantiomers have the same melting point.", "expl": "Enantiomers differ only in a chiral environment and in the direction they rotate plane-polarised light."},
{"text": "Aniline is a stronger base than ammonia.", "expl": "The nitrogen lone pair in aniline is conjugated with the ring, so it accepts a proton less readily."},
{"text": "Fructose gives a positive Fehling's (Trommer's) test.", "expl": "Although it is a ketose, in alkaline conditions it isomerises to aldoses, which reduce copper(II) to red {Cu2O}."},
{"text": "Starch gives a dark blue colour with iodine.", "expl": "Iodine molecules enter the amylose helix, forming a coloured complex; this is the classic test for starch."},
{"text": "Denaturation of a protein breaks its peptide bonds.", "expl": "Denaturation destroys the 3D structure (hydrogen bonds, bridges), but the peptide chain stays intact."},
{"text": "Polystyrene is made by condensation polymerisation.", "expl": "Styrene has a C=C bond and undergoes addition polymerisation with no by-product."},
{"text": "Methanoic (formic) acid gives a positive Tollens' test.", "expl": "Methanoic acid contains an aldehyde-like {H-CO-} group, so it reduces silver ions."},
{"text": "Esterification is an irreversible reaction.", "expl": "Esterification is an equilibrium reaction; the yield is increased by removing water or using an excess of a reactant."},
{"text": "Glycine is optically inactive.", "expl": "The alpha carbon of glycine carries two hydrogen atoms, so it is not a stereocentre."},
{"text": "The molality of a solution does not depend on temperature.", "expl": "Molality refers to the mass of solvent, and mass does not change on heating."},
{"text": "The molar concentration of a solution does not depend on temperature.", "expl": "The volume of a solution changes with temperature, so its molar concentration changes too."},
{"text": "A 1 mol/kg {NaCl} solution boils at a higher temperature than a glucose solution of the same molality.", "expl": "{NaCl} dissociates into 2 ions, so it gives twice as many particles as glucose."},
{"text": "Mist is a liquid aerosol.", "expl": "In mist, water droplets (dispersed phase) are spread through air (gaseous dispersion medium)."},
{"text": "The solubility of most solids in water increases with temperature.", "expl": "Dissolving most salts is endothermic; exceptions include {Ca(OH)2} and {Li2CO3}, whose solubility decreases."},
{"text": "Comparing Ksp values always shows which salt is more soluble, even for salts of different types (e.g. {AB} and {AB2}).", "expl": "Ksp values can be compared directly only for salts of the same type; for different types you must calculate the solubility."},
{"text": "The osmotic pressure of a solution increases with temperature.", "expl": "From π = cRT, osmotic pressure is directly proportional to absolute temperature."},
{"text": "A 10% solution contains 10 g of solute in 100 g of water.", "expl": "Percentage concentration refers to the mass of solution: 10 g of solute in 100 g of solution, i.e. in 90 g of water."},
{"text": "Diluting a solution does not change the number of moles of solute.", "expl": "Only solvent is added, which is why c1V1 = c2V2."},
{"text": "The vapour pressure above a solution of a non-volatile solute is higher than above the pure solvent.", "expl": "By Raoult's law the vapour pressure falls, because solute particles reduce the solvent's share of the solution."},
{"text": "An emulsion is a colloidal system of two immiscible liquids.", "expl": "Milk is an example – fat droplets dispersed in water; emulsifiers stabilise such systems."},
{"text": "Mixing 1 dm³ of ethanol with 1 dm³ of water gives exactly 2 dm³ of solution.", "expl": "The volume contracts – the molecules form hydrogen bonds and pack more tightly, so the total volume is smaller."},
{"text": "The freezing-point depression of a solution is affected by dissociation of the solute.", "expl": "Dissociation increases the number of particles, which the van 't Hoff factor i accounts for in ΔT = i·K·m."},
{"text": "A volumetric flask is calibrated 'to contain' (TC), not 'to deliver'.", "expl": "Filled to the mark, the flask contains its nominal volume, but when emptied some liquid stays on the walls."},
{"text": "A desiccator is used to cool and store substances in a dry atmosphere.", "expl": "A drying agent (e.g. silica gel) stops the sample absorbing moisture before it is weighed."},
{"text": "Sodium oxalate is the primary standard used to standardise {NaOH} solution.", "expl": "Sodium oxalate is used to standardise {KMnO4}; {NaOH} is standardised e.g. with potassium hydrogen phthalate."},
{"text": "A standard {NaOH} solution can be prepared from an accurately weighed portion of solid {NaOH}.", "expl": "Solid {NaOH} absorbs water and {CO2} from the air, so its mass is uncertain – the solution must be standardised."},
{"text": "Absorbance is a dimensionless quantity.", "expl": "Absorbance is the logarithm of a ratio of light intensities (A = log I₀/I), so it has no unit."},
{"text": "Before measuring a solution, a pipette is rinsed with a small amount of that solution.", "expl": "Water left on the walls would dilute the sample, so the pipette is 'conditioned' with the solution being measured."},
{"text": "The calomel electrode is a reference electrode.", "expl": "It has a constant, known potential that does not depend on the composition of the solution being tested."},
{"text": "Precision and accuracy of a measurement mean the same thing.", "expl": "Precision is how well results agree with each other; accuracy is how close they are to the true value."},
{"text": "{KMnO4} solution is stored in dark glass bottles.", "expl": "Light speeds up the decomposition of {KMnO4} to {MnO2}, which changes the solution's concentration."},
{"text": "In thin-layer chromatography, the Rf value can be greater than 1.", "expl": "A substance cannot travel further than the solvent front, so Rf is always between 0 and 1."},
{"text": "A hot crucible is weighed immediately after it is taken out of the furnace.", "expl": "A hot crucible causes convection currents that disturb the weighing; it is first cooled in a desiccator."},
{"text": "Halogenated solvent waste is collected separately from non-halogenated waste.", "expl": "Incinerating halogenated waste needs special conditions, e.g. to limit the formation of dioxins."},
{"text": "EDTA forms 1 : 1 complexes with most metal ions.", "expl": "This makes complexometric calculations simple: n(metal) = n(EDTA)."},
{"text": "In ammonia synthesis, all the feed gas reacts in a single pass through the reactor.", "expl": "Single-pass conversion is low, so after {NH3} is condensed out, the unreacted gases are recycled to the reactor."},
{"text": "In the contact process, {SO2} is oxidised to {SO3} over several catalyst beds, with the gases cooled between them.", "expl": "The reaction is exothermic, so a rise in temperature would lower the equilibrium yield; interstage cooling improves it."},
{"text": "In the Ostwald process, ammonia is catalytically oxidised to nitrogen monoxide.", "expl": "{4NH3 + 5O2 -> 4NO + 6H2O}; then {NO} is oxidised to {NO2}, which reacts with water to give {HNO3}."},
{"text": "In the Solvay process, ammonia is recovered and recycled.", "expl": "{NH4Cl} is heated with milk of lime and the released {NH3} returns to the ammonia absorber."},
{"text": "The membrane in a membrane cell lets {Na^+} cations through but blocks {Cl^-} and {OH^-} anions.", "expl": "It is a cation-exchange membrane – it gives pure {NaOH} free of chloride in the cathode compartment and keeps chlorine away from the alkali."},
{"text": "In a blast furnace, coke is both a fuel and a source of the reducing agent.", "expl": "Burning coke supplies heat and forms {CO}, which reduces the iron oxides."},
{"text": "Hydrotreating of petroleum fractions is used, among other things, to remove sulfur.", "expl": "Hydrogen over a catalyst converts sulfur compounds into {H2S}, which is then separated off."},
{"text": "Portland cement hardens only by drying out.", "expl": "Cement sets through the hydration of calcium silicates with water, not simply by drying."},
{"text": "Teflon (PTFE) is made by polymerising tetrafluoroethene.", "expl": "The monomer {CF2=CF2} undergoes addition polymerisation to give a chemically and thermally resistant polymer."},
{"text": "Ground phosphate rock is a fast-acting fertiliser because it dissolves well in water.", "expl": "{Ca3(PO4)2} is poorly soluble, which is why it is treated with acids to make superphosphate."},
{"text": "Cracking breaks down heavy crude oil fractions into lighter hydrocarbons, e.g. petrol components.", "expl": "Long chains break under heat and a catalyst, increasing the yield of petrol from crude oil."},
{"text": "Counter-current flow increases the efficiency of heat and mass transfer in process equipment.", "expl": "A large temperature or concentration difference is maintained along the whole unit, driving the transfer."}
